{"title":"Cubic formula","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The cubic formula is a very complicated formula used to solve cubics. It is not used very often, as schools don't teach it and problem writers usually hide a simpler tactic instead.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Cardano.27s_formula\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Cardano's_formula\">Cardano's formula</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cubic_formula&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Cardano&#39;s formula\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Start with the cubic <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/c/03c508e27be4f3b85186b98663167ca5419f2902.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$aw^3+bw^2+cw+d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"159\" height=\"18\" />. The constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> controls how steep it is. The constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> shifts it up and down. The constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> controls the slope of the middle. And the constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> shifts it left to right. For now, let's just reduce it to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/e/53e9456c8047826b5eb97b8d10b31597c995bf1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^3+\\frac{b}{a}x^2+\\frac{c}{a}x+\\frac{d}{a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"155\" height=\"39\" />, as everyone knows how to do that. Consider the cubic <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/6/1a6b27e83fa532e772e1b4c90dd24fecdd7a0003.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x+\\frac{b}{3a})^3=x^3+\\frac{b}{a}x^2+\\frac{b^2}{3a^2}x+\\frac{b^3}{27a^3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"299\" height=\"41\" />. The first two terms match, and if we let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/b/41bccce6b7f7e8515b37cbe89b33994cbedd5253.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-\\frac{b}{3a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"57\" height=\"39\" />, we get rid of that annoying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b2878564ab19b80abb21ba964abe90fe120846.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2$\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" /> term. We'll just add it back at the end. Making the substitution results in: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/4/314d897169e3621f933a7910bb85ae1912a4dad8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(x-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)^3+\\frac{b}{a}\\left(x-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)^2+\\frac{c}{a}\\left(x-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)+\\frac{d}{a}=x^3+\\frac{3ac-b^2}{3a^2}x+\\frac{2b^3+27a^2d-9abc}{27a^3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"691\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p><p>By now, let's define <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/c/9cca53f7d808ee06939e9860dd3a851f095fe03c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p=\\frac{3ac-b^2}{3a^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"103\" height=\"41\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/a/48ac64707acbfc6df8c3024ec36f3c43d2ab014d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q=\\frac{2b^3+27a^2d-9abc}{27a^3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"187\" height=\"41\" />. We get the depressed cubic <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/1/7c1e6b78b725efba4dd00dfcebab3a5d18ef96e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^3+px+q=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"126\" height=\"20\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/3/9e372e2896917057e8b6629b2c6eb5e801065721.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^3=-px-q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"109\" height=\"20\" />. First, let's expand <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/e/dbe5800355c5b4973ac57ba760837e6894874b02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(u+v)^3=u^3+3u^2v+3uv^2+v^3=3uv(u+v)+(u^3+v^3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"467\" height=\"21\" />. Now, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/4/7b4bf86e6e84e565265f522ed0406150ef1b3bc1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=u+v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"79\" height=\"14\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/a/e8a094ed5841baf23350fbc9295f2aebfb98967a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(u+v)^3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"66\" height=\"21\" /> matches up with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/5/48524d3889ec8433cb03669176e738011df245b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^3$\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/7/c57ec58a00d14e91d205758b9257e4107908afc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3uv(u+v)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"87\" height=\"20\" /> matches up with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/7/f87cc397abb25eea06ec0c8fde80a5172287a207.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-px$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"16\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/f/7cf710c2e074090e0d1870d88343dd2991c1cdb0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(v^3+w^3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"76\" height=\"21\" /> matches up with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/e/87e618e9677dec5580ce42c0f72ee9fae8ba2de5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"22\" height=\"11\" />. Things are about to get exciting!\n</p><p>Now, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/4/7b4bf86e6e84e565265f522ed0406150ef1b3bc1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=u+v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"79\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/d/edde817eed0c3d95d2ab5aa6f7a0d6a19f5db07e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$uv=-\\frac{p}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"73\" height=\"34\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/c/4cc0d3949aa134e44595a9354ea93e653298e5f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u^3+v^3=-q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"106\" height=\"20\" />. Let's cube p to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/8/63808ed61276a0605ea31b3584bf3a1634039ad6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u^3v^3=-\\frac{p^3}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"97\" height=\"41\" /> and then write the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/6/196423f6151f2636ea631519c1fd15e516df05c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v^3\\cdot -q=v^3(v^3+u^3)=v^6+v^3u^3=v^6-\\frac{p^3}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"351\" height=\"41\" />. This may not sound exciting until our substitution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/e/54e21906a9b2880058becce62d26d717d2e6b885.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w=v^3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"56\" height=\"17\" />. We got rid of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and now we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/4/f5485f18ea1b02db1a7cb98d1893638af1a6fe68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w^2+\\left(q\\right)w+\\left(-\\frac{p^3}{27}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"169\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>Using the quadratic formula, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/4/3442d355cab32d01ff0c23e105507ac200920938.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w=\\frac{-q}{2}+\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{q^2}{4}+\\frac{p^3}{27}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"185\" height=\"45\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/3488cb5e23318ca67e412d573c80c906b16d050b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{-q}{2}+\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{q^2}{4}+\\frac{p^3}{27}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"200\" height=\"56\" /> and by symmetry, so is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. (If this results in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/d/91d7ada08ad200c068f7060c22ead4ad2e1a2e8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{0}{0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />, interpret it as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />).\n</p><p>Now, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/c/55cc637bc6a755f778feb56e64ed9bc8071217cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{-q}{2}+\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{q^2}{4}+\\frac{p^3}{27}}}+\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{-q}{2}+\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{q^2}{4}+\\frac{p^3}{27}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"387\" height=\"56\" /> (The two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/2/91205e81c98c486418902c88d5c396f5355491f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pm$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" />'s need not be the same). As a bonus, if you make the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/2/91205e81c98c486418902c88d5c396f5355491f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pm$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" />'s <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/c/77cf26be132ef93923e082ee4153b2cb0ef44a50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$+$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/d/add22d9bda21e86390bc74fa4dde17730a442da7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, the resulting root is always real because they become complex conjugates.\n</p><p>Substituting back, we get the cubic formula (remember <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc5bccda0ba2b6c708398417084f165cd7b8c5fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{b}{3a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"25\" height=\"39\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/f/c1fde939293d82579ed0cd2c9768e518394a77b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\frac{-2b^3-27a^2d+9abc}{27a^3}}{2}\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2b^3+27a^2d-9abc}{27a^3}\\right)^2}{4}+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3ac-b^2}{3a^2}\\right)^3}{27}}}+\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\frac{-2b^3-27a^2d+9abc}{27a^3}}{2}\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2b^3+27a^2d-9abc}{27a^3}\\right)^2}{4}+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3ac-b^2}{3a^2}\\right)^3}{27}}}-\\frac{b}{3a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"911\" height=\"67\" />. Simplifying, we get: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/8/af845666c207cb2b7d0c8c1870d87d0b2a800f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{-2b^3-27a^2d+9abc}{54a^3}\\pm\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{2b^3+27a^2d-9abc}{54a^3}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{3ac-b^2}{9a^2}\\right)^3}}\\\\+\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{-2b^3-27a^2d+9abc}{54a^3}\\pm\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{2b^3+27a^2d-9abc}{54a^3}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{3ac-b^2}{9a^2}\\right)^3}}\\\\-\\frac{b}{3a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"1052\" height=\"172\" />.\n</p><p>Note that the calculation is easier if you simply calculate the values for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and then substitute into the simpler formula as opposed to calculating their values multiple times in the evaluation of the longer formula.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Disadvantages\">Disadvantages</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cubic_formula&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Disadvantages\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First of all, even with normal cubics, the square rooting step introduces complex numbers, which can make the cube root more complicated. In the end, if you take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/c/77cf26be132ef93923e082ee4153b2cb0ef44a50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$+$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/d/add22d9bda21e86390bc74fa4dde17730a442da7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, they will be conjugates but it will go through a awful mess first. It is possible to make another cubic formula that avoids this insanity, but that requires trig. Next, try applying it on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/d/55db93035772658fa6f2982559fc9a6d80ed3a25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^3-15x-126$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"120\" height=\"18\" />. You'll get the right answer of 6, but you need to go through a lot of stuff first. Finally, apply it on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/a/c1a021aa0acb0b635a9ca8c43e2ee5ff9a7213e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^3-6x-40$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"102\" height=\"18\" />. What did you get? (The answer is 4). You got <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/8/28810ae8f0d594dcefc8c3c40d06308131eb1dab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt[3]{20-\\sqrt{392}}+\\sqrt[3]{20+\\sqrt{392}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"226\" height=\"35\" />, didn't you? Now how is that equal to 4? It is.\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018081844\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.019 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.029 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 261/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 10610/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:27939:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018081844 and revision id 293107. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}